# 📚 Array (Part 2) - Basic Operations on Arrays

## 📌 Introduction

After understanding what an array is, the next step is learning the common operations that can be performed on it.

These operations are the foundation of almost every array-based problem in Data Structures and Algorithms.

---

# 📋 Array Operations

The most common operations on an array are:

- Traversal
- Access
- Update
- Searching
- Insertion
- Deletion

---

# 1️⃣ Traversal

## 📖 Definition

Traversal means visiting every element of an array exactly once.

---

### Example

```text
Array:

10 20 30 40 50
```

Traversal Output

```text
10
20
30
40
50
```

---

### C++ Code

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {10,20,30,40,50};
    int n = 5;

    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
    {
        cout<<arr[i]<<" ";
    }
}
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(n)
```

### Space Complexity

```
O(1)
```

---

# 2️⃣ Accessing Elements

## 📖 Definition

Access means retrieving an element using its index.

Since arrays provide direct memory access, retrieving an element is extremely fast.

---

### Example

```text
Array

Index : 0 1 2 3 4

Value : 10 20 30 40 50
```

Access

```cpp
arr[2]
```

Output

```
30
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(1)
```

---

# 3️⃣ Updating Elements

## 📖 Definition

Updating means replacing the value stored at a particular index.

---

### Example

Before

```text
10 20 30 40 50
```

Update

```cpp
arr[2] = 99;
```

After

```text
10 20 99 40 50
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(1)
```

---

# 4️⃣ Searching

Searching means finding whether a particular element exists in the array.

There are two common searching techniques.

## Linear Search

Checks every element one by one.

Example

```text
Find 40

10 20 30 40 50
```

Found at index **3**

---

### Algorithm

```
Start from index 0

Compare every element

If found

Return index

Else

Return -1
```

---

### C++ Code

```cpp
int search(int arr[],int n,int key)
{
    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
    {
        if(arr[i]==key)
            return i;
    }

    return -1;
}
```

---

### Time Complexity

Best Case

```
O(1)
```

Worst Case

```
O(n)
```

---

# 5️⃣ Insertion

## 📖 Definition

Insertion means adding a new element at a particular position.

To insert an element, all elements after that position must shift one place to the right.

---

### Example

Before

```text
10 20 40 50
```

Insert **30** at index **2**

After

```text
10 20 30 40 50
```

---

### Visualization

```text
10 20 40 50

↓

10 20 30 40 50
```

---

### Time Complexity

Beginning

```
O(n)
```

Middle

```
O(n)
```

End

```
O(1)
```

(Only if free space is available.)

---

# 6️⃣ Deletion

## 📖 Definition

Deletion means removing an element from the array.

After deletion, remaining elements shift one position towards the left.

---

### Example

Before

```text
10 20 30 40 50
```

Delete

```
30
```

After

```text
10 20 40 50
```

---

### Visualization

```text
10 20 30 40 50

↓

10 20 40 50
```

---

### Time Complexity

Beginning

```
O(n)
```

Middle

```
O(n)
```

End

```
O(1)
```

---

# 📊 Complexity Summary

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|-----------|-----------------|
| Access | O(1) |
| Update | O(1) |
| Traversal | O(n) |
| Linear Search | O(n) |
| Insertion | O(n) |
| Deletion | O(n) |

---

# 💡 Interview Tips

✅ Accessing an element in an array is **O(1)** because arrays use index-based addressing.

✅ Insertion and deletion are expensive because elements must be shifted.

✅ Traversal always requires visiting every element.

✅ Linear Search works on both sorted and unsorted arrays.

✅ Binary Search cannot be applied to an unsorted array.

---

# 📝 Summary

- Traversal visits every element once.
- Access and Update are constant-time operations.
- Searching finds an element in an array.
- Insertion requires shifting elements to the right.
- Deletion requires shifting elements to the left.
- Understanding these operations is essential before solving array interview problems.

